# Throwing a non-Error value — a string, number, or object literal — instead
# of `throw new Error(...)` (jsts#37, CWE-397 style). A thrown string carries
# no stack trace, breaks `instanceof Error` handling, and produces useless
# "Uncaught negative not allowed" messages with no origin. ESLint's
# `no-throw-literal` / `@typescript-eslint/only-throw-error` flag exactly this.
#
# Why a NEW rule (verified false-negative on mestjs slop.service.ts,
# 2026-09-26): `if (n < 0) throw 'negative not allowed';` was not detected by
# any existing rule. SEC-040 matches some `throw` lines for verbose-error
# reasons but does not target the literal-vs-Error distinction.
#
# HOW: match `throw` immediately followed by a string literal (single, double,
# or backtick quote) or a bare numeric literal. It deliberately does NOT match
# `throw new ...`, `throw Error(...)`, `throw err`, or `throw { code: ... }`
# style objects — the discriminator is a quote/digit right after `throw`.
#
# RE2-safe: a single character class after a bounded `\s*`; no backreferences
# or nested unbounded quantifiers. WARN, not block: throwing a literal is a
# quality/robustness smell, not an exploitable defect.
#
# @see docs/research/javascript-typescript.md #37, ESLint no-throw-literal.
id: KEUR-ERR-002
maturity: 1
title: Throw of a string/number literal instead of an Error object
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .js .ts .jsx .tsx
  exclude_paths: node_modules/ dist/ build/ vendor/
patterns:
  # `throw` followed by a quote (', ", `) or a leading digit — a literal value
  # with no stack trace. `new`/identifier/`{` after throw are not matched.
  - regex: \bthrow\s+(?:['"`]|[0-9])
    message: "Throwing a string/number literal — no stack trace and breaks `instanceof Error` handling (jsts#37). Use `throw new Error(...)` (or a subclass). @see KEUR-ERR-002"
confidence: 0.8
fix: "Replace `throw 'message'` with `throw new Error('message')`, or a domain-specific `Error` subclass. This preserves the stack trace and keeps `catch (e) { e instanceof Error }` working for callers."
